EXT. DRIVE-IN SCREEN, ABANDONED - LATE AFTERNOON

A rusted frame stands over a field gone to goldenrod. KESTREL "KES" ODUYA (16) lies flat on the hood of a hatchback, shoes off. His sister WILLOW ODUYA (13) sits on the bumper, kicking gravel. Their cousin BASTIEN LEROUX (17) arrives on a bicycle, one wheel ticking.

He drops the bike. She watches him come. Kes doesn't turn his head.

BASTIEN
You said four.

KES
It's four.

BASTIEN
It's four-twenty.

WILLOW
He's been lying there since three.

KES
Thinking.

BASTIEN
About what?

KES
Nothing. That's the point of it.

WILLOW
You said you'd teach me to drive today.

KES
I said maybe.

WILLOW
You said Saturday, and it's Saturday.

BASTIEN
She's got a point.

KES
Nobody asked you.

BASTIEN
You're the one who asked me to come.

KES
(a long pause)
I know.

WILLOW
Is this about Dad?

KES
No.

WILLOW
It's about Dad.

BASTIEN
Willow.

WILLOW
What? He moved out in April. We're allowed to say it.

KES
It's not about Dad. It's about the screen. They used to show movies on it. Did you know that? Mom said they'd park here and nobody watched. They'd just sit.

BASTIEN
Sounds nice.

KES
That's what I mean. It sounds nice.

WILLOW
Can I still sit behind the wheel?

KES
(sitting up, finally)
Come on. Keys are in the visor.

BASTIEN
Do I get a turn?

KES
After her.

WILLOW
Ha.
